Usually: look at the target first, then look at the ball during the actual strike.

For a pass or shot, the sequence is:

scan target → choose line → set plant foot → eyes on ball/contact point → strike → follow through

Right at contact, you generally want your head stable and your eyes on the ball, because clean contact depends on hitting the correct part of the ball with the correct part of your foot. The target is already “loaded” into your motor plan from the glance before the kick.

As skill improves, you need less conscious visual attention on the ball because proprioception and repetition take over, so you can keep more awareness on defenders, teammates, and the goal.

So the principle is: aim with the target, execute on the ball.